okay today I'm going to be talking to  you about gas pressure we're going to  find out what gas pressure is and three  different things which can affect gas  pressure so first of all I'm going to  show it draw you a diagram so this is a  closed container and within it there are  so I'm going to draw four gas particles  so remember in a gas the particles are  spread out and they move randomly in  every direction now obviously if this  was reality there'd be millions and  millions of these particles so as the  particles move they collide with the  walls of the container and as they  collide with the walls of the container  they exert a force now this overall  force is referred to as pressure so  every single collision of all of these  kind of act together and that overall  force exerted on this area here on the  kind of surface area of the container is  called pressure so the first one we need  to know about we're going to learn three  different things that affect pressure  the first one we're going to look at is  the number of particles so let's have a  look at two different situations here  I've got two different containers but  they have the exact same volume now in  the top container let's say I haven't  got many gas particles whereas in the  bottom one I've got many more so what  we're gonna have a quick think about is  which one of these is going to have a  higher pressure now if there's not many  particles there will be fewer collisions  with the outside with the with the walls  of the container if we've got more  particles there will be more frequent  collisions so the more collisions that  there are the higher the pressure will  be so this top one will have a low  pressure whereas this bottom one will  have a high pressure so if we have more  particles within the same volume will  have more collisions or more frequent  collisions therefore a higher pressure  the second one we need to know about is  volume  so again let's have a look at two  different examples so the top one we've  got a quite a large volume compared to  the bottom one so let's make sure we've  got the same number of particles in both  different situations keep it fair now  which one is going to have a higher  pressure this time so we're thinking  about the frequency of collisions  because there's a smaller volume here  there's going to be more collisions or  more frequent collisions than this one  here so therefore there's going to be  more force exerted on the walls of this  container so therefore this one has a  lower pressure and this one has a higher  pressure so as long as you keep the  number of particles the same as you  decrease the volume you will increase  the number of the frequency of  collisions therefore you will increase  the pressure the final one we need to  know about is temperature so we're going  to look at two different situations this  top one here and this bottom one here  now I'm what we're going to do is going  to keep a number of particles the same  so we'll just show four in each one  we've got the same volume each time what  we've changed this time is the  temperature so let's say this top one is  5 degrees C whereas this bottom one is  200 degrees C now when these these  particles are cold they don't have much  kinetic energy so kinetic energy just  means movement energy so let's think  about the force that they're exerting on  the walls of the container because  they're not moving with much kinetic  energy the force is going to be quite  low therefore the pressure will be low  as well  because remember pressure is just the  kind of net force upon the on the  surface area of the inside of this  container now imagine this we heat this  up to 200 degrees Celsius these  particles are going to have much  much much more kinetic energy now so  even though we're not even though we've  got the same volley with the same number  of particles the force that they're  colliding with the inside of this  container is much greater than this one  here also it will be more frequent as  well because they're moving faster so  these two factors result in having high  pressure so just to summarize gas  pressure is all about particles  colliding with the inside of container  if we have more particles in the same  volume will have a higher pressure as we  decrease the volume we increase the  pressure and as we increase the  temperature we're giving the particles  more kinetic energy and therefore we are  increasing the pressure as well thank  you
 

